        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: श्वः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "श्वः — śvaḥ — \"tomorrow\". The time word stands first and the future follows: श्वः सः पठिष्यति। — \"Tomorrow he will read.\" You already have परश्वः, the day after it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "śvaḥ is śvas \"tomorrow\", already in the Rigveda, with the final s said as ḥ at the end of a phrase (MW). Pali kept it as sve \"tomorrow\" (CDIAL 12760); the Hindi kal comes from a different word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
